# Class-Body Instance Attribute Annotations

Declare instance attributes once as **class-body annotations** with no default
value. This surfaces the class's full schema at the top of the definition and
keeps `__init__` focused on wiring, not documenting field types one line at a
time.

```python
# BAD: schema scattered through __init__; must read every line to learn what the class holds
class PredictFutureYearExperiment(BaseExperiment):
    def __init__(self, spec: PredictFutureYearExperimentTrainingSpec, ctx: Context) -> None:
        super().__init__(spec, ctx)
        self.test_year = spec.test_year
        self.val_year = spec.val_year
        self.hold_out_last_n_years = spec.hold_out_last_n_years

# GOOD: schema declared up front; __init__ just wires it
class PredictFutureYearExperiment(BaseExperiment):
    test_year: int | None
    val_year: int | None
    hold_out_last_n_years: int | None

    def __init__(self, spec: PredictFutureYearExperimentTrainingSpec, ctx: Context) -> None:
        super().__init__(spec, ctx)
        self.test_year = spec.test_year
        self.val_year = spec.val_year
        self.hold_out_last_n_years = spec.hold_out_last_n_years
```

## Rules

- **No default value on the annotation.** Use `x: T`, not `x: T = None`. A
  default creates a real class attribute that subclasses silently inherit,
  hiding cases where `__init__` forgot to set it. The annotation-only form
  fails loudly with `AttributeError`.
- **No need to repeat the annotation inline.** Once `x: set[str] | None` is
  declared on the class, `self.x = None` in `__init__` type-checks fine — drop
  the inline `self.x: set[str] | None = None`.
- **Declare on the class that owns the attribute.** A subclass re-declares only
  the attributes it *adds*, never ones inherited from a parent.
- **Skip dataclasses.** Their fields are already class-body annotated by the
  `@dataclass` decorator; adding parallel annotations breaks the mechanism.
  (Applies to e.g. `BaseTrainingSpec`, `Splits`, `Dataset`.)
